#: ../../../addin.md:1
msgid "Add-in & Settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:5
msgid ""
"The xlwings add-in is the preferred way to be able to use the `Run main` "
"button, `RunPython` or `UDFs`. Note that you don't need an add-in if you "
"just want to manipulate Excel by running a Python script."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:9
msgid ""
"The ribbon of the add-in is compatible with Excel >= 2007 on Windows and >= "
"2016 on macOS.   On macOS, all UDF related functionality is not available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:14
msgid ""
"The add-in is password protected with the password `xlwings`. For debugging "
"or to add new extensions, you need   to unprotect it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:18
msgid "Run main"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:23
msgid ""
"The `Run main` button is the easiest way to run your Python code: It runs a "
"function called `main` in a Python module that has the same name as your "
"workbook. This allows you to save your workbook as `xlsx` without enabling "
"macros. The `xlwings quickstart` command will create a workbook that will "
"automatically work with the `Run` button."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:27
msgid "Installation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:29
msgid "To install the add-in, use the command line client:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:35
msgid ""
"Technically, this copies the add-in from Python's installation directory to "
"Excel's `XLSTART` folder. Then, to use `RunPython` or `UDFs` in a workbook, "
"you need to set a reference to `xlwings` in the VBA editor, see screenshot "
"(Windows: `Tools > References...`, Mac: it's on the lower left corner of the "
"VBA editor). Note that when you create a workbook via `xlwings quickstart`, "
"the reference should already be set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:39
msgid "User Settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:41
msgid ""
"When you install the add-in for the first time, it will get auto-configured "
"and therefore, a `quickstart` project should work out of the box. For fine-"
"tuning, here are the available settings:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:43
msgid ""
"`Interpreter`: This is the path to the Python interpreter. This works also "
"with virtual or conda envs on Mac. If you use conda envs on Windows, then "
"leave this empty and use `Conda Path` and `Conda Env` below instead. "
"Examples: `\"C:\\Python39\\pythonw.exe\"` or `\"/usr/local/bin/python3.9\"`. "
"Note that in the settings, this is stored as `Interpreter_Win` or "
"`Interpreter_Mac`, respectively, see below!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:47
msgid ""
"`PYTHONPATH`: If the source file of your code is not found, add the path to "
"its directory here."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:48
msgid ""
"`Conda Path`: If you are on Windows and use Anaconda or Miniconda, then type "
"here the path to your installation, e.g. `C:\\Users\\Username\\Miniconda3` "
"or `%USERPROFILE%\\Anaconda`. NOTE that you need at least conda 4.6! You "
"also need to set `Conda Env`, see next point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:51
msgid ""
"`Conda Env`: If you are on Windows and use Anaconda or Miniconda, type here "
"the name of your conda env, e.g. `base` for the base installation or `myenv` "
"for a conda env with the name `myenv`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:53
msgid ""
"`UDF Modules`: Names of Python modules (without .py extension) from which "
"the UDFs are being imported. Separate multiple modules by \";\". Example: "
"`UDF_MODULES = \"common_udfs;myproject\"` The default imports a file in the "
"same directory as the Excel spreadsheet with the same name but ending in `."
"py`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:57
msgid ""
"`Debug UDFs`: Check this box if you want to run the xlwings COM server "
"manually for debugging, see [Debugging](debugging.md#debugging)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:58
msgid ""
"`RunPython: Use UDF Server`:  Uses the same COM Server for RunPython as for "
"UDFs. This will be faster, as the interpreter doesn't shut down after each "
"call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:60
msgid "`Restart UDF Server`: This restarts the UDF Server/Python interpreter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:61
msgid ""
"`Show Console`: Check the box in the ribbon or set the config to `TRUE` if "
"you want the command prompt to pop up. This currently only works on Windows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:62
msgid ""
"`ADD_WORKBOOK_TO_PYTHONPATH`: Uncheck this box to not automatically add the "
"directory of your workbook to the PYTHONPATH. This can be helpful if you "
"experience issues with OneDrive/SharePoint: uncheck this box and provide the "
"path where your source file is manually via the PYTHONPATH setting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:64
msgid "Anaconda/Miniconda"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:66
msgid ""
"If you use Anaconda or Miniconda on Windows, you will need to set your "
"`Conda Path` and `Conda Env` settings, as you will otherwise get errors when "
"using `NumPy` etc. In return, leave `Interpreter` empty."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:69
msgid "Making use of Environment Variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:71
msgid ""
"With environment variables, you can set dynamic paths e.g. to your "
"interpreter or `PYTHONPATH`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:73
msgid ""
"On Windows, you can use all environment variables like so: `%USERPROFILE%"
"\\Anaconda`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:74
msgid ""
"On macOS, the following special variables are supported: `$HOME`, "
"`$APPLICATIONS`, `$DOCUMENTS`, `$DESKTOP`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:76
msgid "Config Hierarchy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:78
msgid "xlwings looks for settings in the following locations and order:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:80
msgid "**Workbook configuration**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:82
msgid ""
"First, xlwings looks for a sheet called `xlwings.conf`. This is the "
"recommended way to configure your workbook for deployment as you don't have "
"to handle an additional config file. When you run the quickstart command, it "
"will create a sample configuration on a sheet called `_xlwings.conf`: remove "
"the leading underscore in the name to activate it. If you don't want to use "
"it, feel free to delete the sheet."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:84
msgid "**Directory configuration**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:86
msgid ""
"Next, xlwings looks for a file called `xlwings.conf` in the same directory "
"as your Excel workbook."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:88
msgid "**User configuration**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:90
msgid ""
"Finally, xlwings looks for a file called `xlwings.conf` in the `.xlwings` "
"folder in the user's home directory. Normally, you don't edit this file "
"directly—instead, it is created and edited by the add-in whenever you change "
"a setting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:92
msgid "You will find more details about the each configuration type below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:94
msgid ""
"*Source: The section \"Config Hierarchy\" is taken from \"Python for Excel "
"by Felix Zumstein (O'Reilly). Copyright 2021 Zoomer Analytics LLC, "
"978-1-492-08100-5.\"*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:96
msgid "User Config: Ribbon/Config File"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:98
msgid ""
"The settings in the xlwings Ribbon are stored in a config file that can also "
"be manipulated externally. The location is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:100
msgid ""
"Windows: `.xlwings\\xlwings.conf` in your home folder, that is usually `C:"
"\\Users\\<username>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:101
msgid "macOS: `~/Library/Containers/com.microsoft.Excel/Data/xlwings.conf`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:103
msgid ""
"The format is as follows (currently the keys are required to be all caps) - "
"note the OS specific Interpreter settings!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:125
msgid ""
"The `ONEDRIVE_WIN/_MAC` setting has to be edited directly in the file, there "
"is currently no possibility to edit it via the ribbon. Usually, it is only "
"required if you are either on macOS or if your environment variables on "
"Windows are not correctly set or if you have a private and corporate "
"location and don't want to go with the default one. `ONEDRIVE_WIN/_MAC` has "
"to point to the root folder of your local OneDrive folder."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:128
msgid "Directory Config: Config file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:130
msgid ""
"The global settings of the Ribbon/Config file can be overridden for one or "
"more workbooks by creating a `xlwings.conf` file in the workbook's directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:134
msgid ""
"Workbook directory config files are not supported if your workbook is stored "
"on SharePoint or OneDrive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:137
msgid "Workbook Config: xlwings.conf Sheet"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:139
msgid ""
"Workbook specific settings will override global (Ribbon) and workbook "
"directory config files:  Workbook specific settings are set by listing the "
"config key/value pairs in a sheet with the name `xlwings.conf`. When you "
"create a new project with `xlwings quickstart`, it'll already have such a "
"sheet but you need to rename it from `_xlwings.conf` to `xlwings.conf` to "
"make it active."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:146
msgid "Alternative: Standalone VBA module"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:148
msgid ""
"Sometimes, it might be useful to run xlwings code without having to install "
"an add-in first. To do so, you need to use the `standalone` option when "
"creating a new project: `xlwings quickstart myproject --standalone`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../addin.md:151
msgid ""
"This will add the content of the add-in as a single VBA module so you don't "
"need to set a reference to the add-in anymore. It will also include "
"`Dictionary.cls` as this is required on macOS. It will still read in the "
"settings from your `xlwings.conf` if you don't override them by using a "
"sheet with the name `xlwings.conf`."
msgstr ""
